\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Counting and eigenchart Gaussian densities}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{Q} be a [positive precision parameter](fdip-0004) on
#{(X_V,h_V)}. Choose an indicator enumeration and its
counting chart #{C:X_V\to\mathbb R^n} from \ref{fdip-002J}. For the
counting array #{A=CQC^{-1}} in \ref{fdip-002O}, the action and volume give
##{\begin{aligned}
S_Q(x)&=\tfrac12(Cx)^TA(Cx),\\
C_*\lambda_V&=\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,n\}}.
\end{aligned}}
Consequently the pushforward of the [Gaussian law](fdip-0005) has
density
##{Z_Q^{-1}\exp(-y^TAy/2)}
against product Lebesgue measure.
The customary expression
##{Z_Q^{-1}\exp(-\phi^TQ\phi /2)}
uses
#{\phi=Cx} and the same symbol #{Q} for this counting array.}
\p{More generally, let #{Q} be a positive precision parameter on a
finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}.
Choose an orthonormal eigenbasis #{u_i} from \ref{fdip-002R}, with
#{Qu_i=q_i u_i}, #{q_i>0}, and use the chart
#{D(x)=(h(u_i,x))_i}. The [canonical-volume characterization](fdip-002M)
makes #{D_*\lambda_E} product Lebesgue measure. In coordinates
#{y=D(x)}, the normalization formula in \ref{fdip-002T} and
scalar integral in \ref{fdip-002S} give
##{\begin{aligned}
w_Q(x)&=\prod_i e^{-q_i y_i^2/2},\\
Z_Q&=\prod_i I(q_i),\qquad I(q_i)=\sqrt{2\pi /q_i}.
\end{aligned}}
Dividing the first product by the second gives the density
##{\prod_i I(q_i)^{-1}e^{-q_i y_i^2/2}.}
This identification uses the scalar value and normalization before
computing covariance. For the empty eigenbasis both products are one,
and the coordinate space is a singleton.}
\p{These chart calculations recover the density convention in
\citet{Lemma B.54 and Proposition B.55, pp. 504-505}{friedli2017statistical}
with precision #{Q}; the source uses covariance #{Q^{-1}}.
Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr} and
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_ofLp} identify the orthonormal
Euclidean-space chart with the ordinary real coordinate product
volume.}
